    <h1>Building the Commercial Internet</h1>
    <p class="case-lead">From a 64 Kbps international connection and four customers to the foundations of a commercial Internet business in South Africa.</p>
    <div class="case-meta"><span>Period</span><strong>1993–1996</strong><span>Organisation</span><strong>TICSA / Internet Africa</strong></div>
  </div>
  <section class="shell case-grid">
    <article>
      <div class="eyebrow">Starting point</div>
      <h2>The early commercial Internet was infrastructure, operations and experimentation.</h2>
      <p>The original account describes launching with three partners, a single 64 Kbps link to UUNET in the United States and four original customers. The work was hands-on: network equipment, connectivity, customer installation and the practical operation of an emerging service.</p>
      <p>Technology choices included Cisco routing, Frame Relay connectivity and early Internet services. The story captures an environment where building the service and figuring out the business happened simultaneously.</p>
    </article>
